	1. Reviewing Basic Networking Concepts
		a. Basic Networking Protocols
			§ Some of the transmission control protocol/Internet Protocol(TCP/IP) protocols provide basic connectivity. Other protocols such as HTTP,SMTP support specific types of traffic.
			§ TCP/IP is suite of protocols.
			§ TCP/IP traffic is either connection oriented TCP traffic or connectionless UDP
			§ TCP - Transmission Control Protocol
				□ Connection oriented protocol (syn, syn-ack, ack)
				□ Three way handshake needed for connection to establish
				
				
				
			§ UDP - User Datagram Protocol
				□ Whole TCP provides guaranteed delivery, UDP makes best effort to deliver traffic without extra traffic to ensure delivery.
				□ ICMP traffic like ping, audio, video streaming use UDP
				□ Many Dos attacks use UDP.
			§ IP - Internet Protocol
				□ It identifies host on TCP/IP network and delivers traffic from one host to another based on IP
			§ ICMP -  Internet Control Protocol
				□ Used for testing basic connectivity and includes tools like ping, pathping, and tracert.
				□ As it is commonly used in attacks on network to discover the devices, ping is generally disabled on firewalls and routers.
			§ ARP - Address Resolution Protocol
				□ Resolves IPv4 to media access control(MAC) addresses.
				□ TCP/IP uses IP address to get a packet to a destination network, but once it arrives on the destination network, it uses MAC address to get it to correct host
				□ ARP is required once packet reaches the destination subnet
			§ NDP -  Neighbor Discovery Protocol
				□ It performs several functions on IPv6.
				□ It performs functions similar to  IPv4's ARP
				□ It also performs auto configuration of IPv6 addresses and discovers other IPv6 devices on the network.
		b. Implementing Protocols for Use Cases
			Networks don't automatically support all the available protocols. Instead, IT professionals identify a need based on the organization goal and enable the best protocol to meet the need.
			i. Voice and Video Use Case
				□ The Real Time Transport Protocol (RTP) delivers audio and video over IP networks. This includes VoIP (Voice over Internet Protocol) communications, streaming media, video teleconferencing etc.
				□ The Secure Real-Time Transport Protocol provides encryption, message authentication and integrity for RTP.
				□ SRTP helps protect confidentiality of data from these attacks while also ensuring the integrity of the  data transmission. This provides protection against replay attacks. (in replay attack, an attacker captures data sent between two entities, modify it, and then attempts to impersonate one of the parties.)
				□ Can be used in both unicast and multicast.
			ii. File Transfer Use Case
				□ Data-in-transit = data over network, data-at-rest = data stored on any type of medium.
				□ Common use cases related to transferring are transmit data over network, ensure confidentiality, ensure administrators connect to server using secure connections
				□ Following are basic protocols which are used to transfer data over network.
					® FTP (File Transfer Protocol)
						◊ Uploads and downloads large files to and from an FTP server.
						◊ By default data transmission is in clear text
						◊ FTP active mode uses TCP port 21 for control signals and TCP port 20 for data.
						◊ FTP Passive mode (PASV) uses TCP port 21 for control signals and random TCP port for data
						◊ If FTP traffic is going through firewall, random port is often blocked, so it is best to disable PASV in FTP clients.
					® TFTP (Trivial File Transfer Protocol)
						◊ It uses UDP port 69 and is used to transfer smaller amount of data
						◊ As it has been used in many attacks, and it is not an essential protocol, it is commonly disabled.
				□ The following list identifies several encryption protocols used to encrypt data-in-transit
					® SSH (Secure Shell)
						◊ It encrypts traffics in transit and can be used to encrypt other protocols such as FTP
						◊ SSH is more secure as compared to telnet, as telnet sends clear text data over network.
						◊ Secure Copy (SCP) is based on SSH and is used to copy encrypted files over a network.
						◊ SSH can also encrypt TCP wrappers.
						◊ When SSH encrypts traffic, it uses TCP port 22.
					® SSL (Secure Socket Layer Protocol)
						◊ It was primary method used to secure HTTP traffic as HTTPS
						◊ It can also encrypt other traffic types like SMTP, LDAP
						◊ As it has been compromised, it is not recommended for use anymore
					® TLS (Transport Layer Security Protocol)
						◊ It is the designated replacement for SSL and should be used instead of the SSL
						◊ Many protocols  that support the TLS use STARTTLS
					® IPsec (Internet Protocol Security)
						◊ It is used to encrypt IP traffic.
						◊ It is native to IPv6 but also work with IPv4
						◊ It encapsulates and encrypts IP packet payloads and uses Tunnel mode to protect virtual private network (VPN) traffic
						◊ It has 2 main components: Authentication Header(AH) identified by protocol ID number 51 and Encapsulating Header(ESP) identified by protocol ID number 50.
						◊ It uses Internet Key Exchange (IKE) over UDP port 500 to create a security association for VPN
					® SFTP (Secure File Transfer Protocol)
						◊ It is secure implementation of FTP with SSH to transmit the file in encrypted format
						◊ It transmits data using TCP port 22
					® FTPS (File Transfer Protocol Secure)
						◊ It is an extension of FTP and uses TLS to encrypt FTP traffic.
						◊ Some implementations of FTPS use TCP ports 989 and 990. However, TLS can also encrypt the traffic over the ports used by FTP (20 and 21). Notice that the difference between SFTP and FTPS is that SFTP uses SSH and FTPS uses TLS.
						
					® Secure Shell (SSH) encrypts traffic over TCP port 22. Transport Layer Security (TLS) is a replacement for SSL and is used to encrypt many different protocols. Secure FTP (SFTP) uses SSH to encrypt traffic. FTP Secure (FTPS) uses TLS to encrypt traffic.
					
			iii. Email and Web Use Cases
