			viii. Domain Name Resolution Use Case
				□ Primary use case is domain name resolution, host to IP.
				□ 
					
				□ It uses UDP port 53
				□ Sometimes, the DNS server you query knows the answer and just gives you the answer, other times, it queries one or more other DNS servers to get the answer and then puts the answer into its own cache for next time use.
				□ DNS servers host data in zones like databases. Zones include multiple records like
					® A -also called host record which holds host name and IPv4 addresses. A DNS client queries DNS with name using a forward lookup request and DNS responds with IPv4 address from record.
					® AAAA - it holds host name and IPv6 address and similar to A record.
					® PTR - also called pointer record and I opposite of an A record. Instead of a DNS client querying DNS with the name, the DNS client queries DNS with the IP address. When configured to do so, the DNS server responds with the name. PTR records are optional, so these reverse lookups do not always work.
					® MX - also called mail exchange or mail exchanger. An MX record identifies a mail server used for email. The MX record is linked to A or AAAA record of mail server.
					® CNAME - A canonical name or alias, allows a single system to have multiple names associated with single IP address.
					® SOA - Start of the Authority record includes information about the DNS zone and some its setting. e.g. TTL settings for DNS
				□ Most DNS server on internet run Berkeley Internet Name Domain (BIND)software and run unix servers. But in Microsoft networks, DNS servers commonly use the Microsoft DNS software.
				□ Occasionally, DNS servers share information with each other in a process known as a zone transfer. In most cases, a zone transfer only includes a small number of updated records. However, some transfers include all the records in the zone. DNS servers use TCP port 53 for zone transfers. In contrast, name resolution queries use UDP port 53.
				□ DNSSEC
					® Onw risk with DNS is DNS Poisoning, also known as DNS cache Poisoning
					® When successful, attackers modify the DNS cache with bogus IP address.
					® One of the primary methods of preventing DNS cache poisoning is with Domain Name System Security Extensions (DNSSEC).
					® DNSSEC is a suite of extensions to DNS that provides validations for DNS responses.
					® It adds digital signature to each record that provides data integrity.
					® If server receives a DNSSEC-enabled response with digitally signed records, the DNS server knows that the response is valid
				□ DNS zones include records such as A records for IPv4 addresses and AAAA records for IPv6 addresses. DNS uses TCP port 53 for zone transfers and UDP port 53 for DNS client queries. Most Internet-based DNS servers run BIND software on Unix or Linux servers, and it’s common to configure DNS servers to only use secure zone transfers. DNSSEC helps prevent DNS poisoning attacks. Nslookup and dig are two command-line tools used to test DNS. Microsoft systems include nslookup; Linux systems include dig.
				□ Nslookup and dig
					® It is used for troubleshooting problems related to DNS. e.g. to verify that DNS server can resolve specific host name or FQDN(fully qualifies domain name - myhost.example.com)
					® The dig commanond-line tool has replaces the nslookup on linux systems. You can dig every DNS server to test if its reachable and can resolve names to IP addresses.
					® Some versions of both commands support the @ symbol to  identify a specific DNS server you want to query. This is useful if you want to pull all the records from a DNS zone. When doing this, you would use the any switch (indicating all records) or the axfr switch (short for all transfer). However, most DNS servers are configured to block these queries.
					
