		c. Understanding and Identifying Ports
			§ Ports are logical numbers used by TCP/IP to identify what services or application should handle data received by a system.
			§ Both TCP and UDP use ports with total of 65,536 TCP ports and 65,536 UDP ports (In TCP segment header, dest/src port bit length)
			§ The Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) divided the ports into three ranges, as follows:
				□ Well-known ports: 0-1023 for commonly used protocols
				□ Registered Ports: 1024-49,151for companies as convenience to IT community. Single company or multiple companies may use same port for specific purpose or can have proprietary
				□ Dynamic and private ports: 49,152-65,535 available for use by any application. Commonly used to temporarily map an application to a port.
				□ Well-known ports are at much higher risk of attacks and port scanner checks if well-known port is open.
			i. Combining the IP Address and the Port
				□ TCP/IP uses the IP address to get the packet to the computer. The computer then uses the port number to get the packet to the correct service, protocol, or application that can process it.
			ii. IP Address Used to Locate Hosts
				□ The server's IP address is used to get the requesting packet from your computer to the server. 
				□ The server gets the response packets back to your computer using your IP address(or IP address of your NAT server)
			iii. Server Ports
				□ Different protocols are enables and running on a server and use well-known registered ports.
				□ When system receives traffic with destination of port 80, system knows to send it to the service handling HTTP
				□ Any web browser knows that the well-known port for HTTP is 80 and even though you don't see port 80 in URL, it is implied by default.
				□ Popular web servers on the Internet include Apache and Internet Information Services (IIS). Apache is free and runs on Unix or Linux systems. Apache can also run on other platforms, such as Microsoft systems. IIS is included in Microsoft Server products. These web servers use port 80 for HTTP. When the server receives a packet with a destination port of 80, the server sends the packet to the web server application (Apache or IIS) that processes it and sends back a response.
			iv. Client Ports
				□ TCP/IP works with the client operating system to maintain a table of client-side ports. This table associates port numbers with different applications that are expecting return traffic. Client-side ports start at port 49,152 and increment up to 65,535. If the system uses all the ports between 49,152 and 65,535 before being rebooted, it’ll start over at 49,152.
				□ When you use your web browser to request a page from a site, your system will record an unused client port number such as 49,152 in an internal table to handle the return traffic. When the web server returns the web page, it includes the client port as a destination port. When the client receives web page packets with a destination port of 49,152, it sends these packets to the web browser application. The browser processes the packets and displays the page.
				
			v. Putting It All Together
				□ 
						
				□ Comparing Ports and Protocol Numbers 
					® Ports and protocol numbers are not the same thing.
					® e.g. IPSec, protocol number 50 indicates packet is an Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) and protocol number 51 indicates it's an Authentication header (AH) packet.
					® You can use protocol number to block or allow traffic on routers and firewalls.
					® Note that it is not accurate to say that you can allow Ipsec ESP traffic by opening port 50 (as IANA lists port 50 as Remote Mail checking protocol). However, you can allow Ipsec traffic by allowing traffic using  protocol number 50.
			vi. The Importance of Ports in Security
				□ Different network devices filter out packets based on IP addresses, ports, and some protocols. As many protocols use well-known ports, you can control protocol traffic by allowing or blocking traffic based on the port.
				□ Web-client-server example, client firewall must allow outgoing traffic on port 80. Firewalls automatically determines the client ports used for return traffic, and if the allow outgoing traffic, they allow return traffic. 
				□ Client firewall doesn't need to allow traffic on port 80 for this work as client isn't hosting web server with HTTP. But, web-server firewall needs to allow incoming traffic on port 80.
				□ Allow traffics on ports which are required.
				
	2. Understanding Basic Network Devices
		○ Any deice on network with IP address is a host/client/node.
		○ Switch connects hosts within a network. And router connects multiple networks together to create larger and larger networks.
		○ Unicast - one-to-one
		○ Multicast - one-to-many
		○ Broadcast - one-to-all, switches pass broadcast traffic within their ports but routers do not pass broadcast traffic.
		a. Switches
			§ A switch can learn which computers are attached to each of its physical ports. It then uses this knowledge to create internal switched connections when two computers communicate with each other.
			§ 
							
			§ When switch is turns on, it starts out without any knowledge other than knowing it has 4 physical ports.
			§ When Lisa sends 1st packet to Homer, it includes MAC of destination computer. However, as switch doesn't know which port Homer's computer is connected to, it forwards this first packet to all the ports on the switch.
			§ Included in the first packet is the MAC address of Lisa's computer. The switch logs this information into an internal table. It then directs any future traffic addressed to Lisa's MAC address to port 1 only.
			§ When Homer's computer receives the packet, it responds. Embedded in this return packet is the MAC address of the Homer's computer. The switch captures Homer's MAC address and logs it with port 4 in the internal table. From here on. Any unicast traffic between Lisa and Homer's computer is internally switched between only ports 1 and 4.
			§ Switches will internally switch unicast traffic. However pass broadcast to all ports.
			
			i. Security Benefit of a Switch
				□ If attacker installed a protocol analyzer on a computer attached to another port e.g. 3, the protocol analyzer can't capture the traffic that doesn't reach the port.
				□ In contrast, if the computers were connected with a simple hub, the attacker could capture it because unicast traffic goes to all ports in a hub.
				□ Hence switch reduces the risk of an attacker capturing the data with a protocol analyzer and also it is efficient.
			ii. Port Security
				□ Port security limits to the computers that can connect to physical ports in a switch.
				□ At basic level, administrator disables the unused ports.
				□ MAC address filtering is the another kind of port security example. In this switch only remembers first1-2 MAC that connect to a port and discards the rest traffic. You can configure this manually to each port to accept traffic only from a specific MAC address. Though manual task labor intensive, it  provides more security.
			iii. Physical Security of a Switch
				□ Many switches has console port that administrators use to monitor the traffic as unlike rest of the switch ports, it can monitor all the traffic in or out of the switch.
				□ If not properly protected, it can also be useful to an attacker.
				□ Physical security ensures that attackers don't have physical access to the switch and other network devices by means of keeping it safe in locked wiring closet.
			iv. Loop Prevention
				□ Loop protection such as STP (Spanning Tree Protocol) and RSTP (Rapid STP) is necessary to protect against switching loop problems, such as those caused when two ports of switch are connected together.
				□ If a user connects 2 of switch ports together with a cable, it creates a switching loop where the switch continuously sends and resends unicast transmissions through the switch. In addition to disabling the switch, it also degrades the overall network performance.
			v. Flood Attacks and Flood Guards
				□ A MAC flood attack attempts to overload a switch with different MAC addresses associated with each physical port. You typically have only one device connected to any physical port and switch 's internal table stores the MAC address associated with this devices and maps it to port.
				□ At some point in MAC flood attack, the switch runs out of the  memory to store all the MAC addresses and enters fail-open state and starts acting like a simple hub and starts transmitting all the packets to all the ports. At this instance attacker can use protocol analyzer to capture all the packets.
				□ Many switches use flood guard to protect against this attack which when enabled, switch will limit the amount if the memory used ti store MAC addresses.
				□ Flood guard simply sends SNMP(Simple Network Management Protocol) trap or error message in response to alert and can disable the port or restrict the activity on the port.
				□ Some switches  set maximum number of MACs supported by a port to 1. however, if a VM is running within a physical host and VM is set to bridged, it can access the network using the physical host's NIC, but with the MAC address of VM. Hence, in such scenario, maximum MAC setting should be set to 2.
		b. Routers
			§ Router connects multiple network segments together into a single network and routes traffic between segments. e.g. internet is effectively a single network hosting billions of computers. Routers route traffic from segment to segment.
			§ As Routers don’t pass broadcast, they effectively reduce network traffic on segment.
			§ Segments are separated by routers are also called broadcast domain.
			§ Too many computers on a single network can result in excessive collisions. Hence moving computers to different segments  significantly improve overall performance. 
			§ Subnetting networks creates separate broadcast domains.
			
			i. Routers and ACLs
				□ ACL are rules implemented on router and on firewalls to allow/reject traffic
				□ Router ACL provides basic packet filtering
				□ It can filter based on IP, ports and some protocol numbers
			ii. Implicit Deny
				□ Implicit Deny indicates that all traffic that isn't explicitly allowed is implicitly denied.
				□ The implicit deny rule is the last rule in an ACL. Some devices automatically apply the implicit deny rule as the last rule. Other devices require an administrator to place the rule at the end of the ACL manually.
				□ Syntax of an implicit deny rule varies on different systems, but it might be something like DENY ANY ANY or DENY ALL ALL.
				
			iii. Antispoofing
				□ Spoofing = to impersonate or masquerade as someone or something else. In this context, an attacker will spoof the source IP address by replacing the actual source IP address with different one to hide the actual source of the packet.
				□ You can implement antispoofing  on a router by modifying the access list to allow or block IP addresses. e.g. private IP addresses traffic coming on internet is obviously an attempt to spoof the source IP.
				• deny ip 10.0.0.0 0.255.255.255 any
				• deny ip 172.16.0.0 0.15.255.255 any
				• deny ip 192.168.0.0 0.0.255.255 any
				
				Antispoofing methods block traffic using the ACL rules.
				
		c. Bridge
			§ A bridge connects multiple networks together and can be used instead of a router in some situations.
			§ Bridge directs traffic based on the destination MAC address.
			§ 
					
			§ The bridge sends traffic to the appropriate segment based on the MAC address.
			§ Similar to switch, bridge learns MAC addresses in different bridge networks by analyzing traffic.
			§ e.g. when Bart in  subnet A sends traffic to Lisa in subnet C, when bridge receives the traffic, it doesn't know if Lisa's MAC address is in segment 1 or segment 2, so it sends traffic to both the bridge networks. When Lisa's computer replies, the bridge identifies her MAC and stores this information in an internal table for next use and then next time sends traffic intended for her, only to her based on this internal table.
			
		d. Aggregation Switch
			§ It connects multiple switches together in a network.
			§ In previous example if we replace bridge with a switch, the switch is an aggregation switch.
			§ Similarly you can connect multiple switches to aggregation switch instead of connecting those to the router, this reduces number of ports used in router.
			§ It's common to place an aggregation switch in the same location as you'd place router using physical security.
			
		e. Firewalls
			§ It filters incoming and outgoing traffic for a single host or between networks.
			§ Firewalls start with a basic routing capability for packet filtering with implicit deny rule. More advanced firewalls go beyond filtering and include advanced content filtering.
			i. Host-Based Firewalls
				□ Host based firewalls provide protection for individual hosts, such as servers or workstations.
				□ A host based firewall provides intrusions protection for the host.
				□ Linux systems support xtables for firewall capabilities.
				□ Network-based firewalls are often dedicated servers or appliances and provide protection for the network.
				
			ii. Application-Based Versus Network-Based Firewalls
				□ It is typically a software running on a system. e.g. host-based firewall is commonly an application based and network-based firewall is usually a dedicated system with additional software installed monitor, filter, and log traffic.
				□ Network-based firewall would have two or more network interfaces cards(NICs) and all traffic would go through firewall.
				□ Most organizations include at least one network-based firewall at the border, between their intranet and internet.
				
			iii. Stateless Firewall Rules
				□ Similar to router rules are implemented in ACL to identify allowed and blocked traffic.
				□ e.g. general format of ACL is 
					® Permission Protocol Source Destination Port
					® ALLOW/DENY TCP/UDP SRC_IP DEST_IP 80
				□ Syntax of these commands varies depending on the device. Some firewalls require you to include a subnet mask in the rule. Some support codes such as www for HTTP traffic, some support keywords like eq for equal, lt for less than etc.
				
			iv. Stateful Versus Stateless
				□ A stateful firewall inspects traffic and makes decision based on the context, or state of the traffic. It also keeps track of established sessions and inspects traffic based on its state within a session. e.g. TCP traffic without handshake will be blocked.
				□ A common security issue with stateless firewalls is misconfigured ACL also missed implicit deny rule.
				
			v. Web Application Firewall
				□ A web application firewall is a firewall specifically designed to protect a web application which commonly hosted on web server.
				□ It is located between server hosting web-application and a client.
				□ It can be stand-alone appliance or software added to another device
				□ e.g. an e-commerce web site would have its web server in DMZ, and it needs more protection. So you wouldn't use WAF in place of network-based firewall, instead, it provides additional layer of protection than only network-based firewall.
				
