				□ DMZ provides access to the services hosted in the DMZ while segmenting access to the internal network.
				□ in figure that the intranet includes a database server. The web server may use this to create web pages for an e-commerce site. It could hold product data, customer data, and much more. FW2 allows traffic between the web server (and only the web server) and the database server on port 1433. FW2 would block all other Internet traffic to the database server.
				□ It’s also possible for the web server and the database server to be part of an extranet. For example, imagine that the web server hosts a site that business partners can use to place orders. The web server would first authenticate them before granting them full access. After users log on, the web site connects to the back-end database server, allowing them to browse the inventory and place orders. Because this site is only for authorized business partners, it is an extranet.
				
			ii. Understanding NAT and PAT
				□ NAT - Network Address Translation
				□ PAT - Port Address Translation
				□ Benefits of NAT include:
					® Public IP addressed don't need to be purchased for all clients
					® NAT hides internal computers from the internet
				□ Only drawback of NAT is that it is not compatible with Ipsec.
				□ There are 2 types of  NAT
					® Static NAT - it uses single public IP address in a one-to-one  mapping. It maps a private IP address with a single public IP address.
					® Dynamic NAT - it uses multiple public IP addresses in one-to -many mapping. Dynamic NAT decides which public IP address to use based on load from public IP pool available. e.g. if several users are connected to the internet on one public IP address, NAT maps the next request to a less-used public IP address with their Port number.
				□ A common form of NAT is Port address Translation.
				□ Private Network Classes
					® Class A -  10.0.0.0-10.255.255.255 subnet mask - 8 bits
					® Class B - 172.16.0.0-172.31.0.0 subnet mask - 16 bits
					® Class C - 192.168.0.0-192.168.255.255  subnet mask -24bits
					
		b. Network Separation
			§ It is common security practice to use different components to provide network separation like segregation, segmentation, and isolation.
			§ Segregation provides basic separation, segmentation refers to putting traffic on different segments, and isolation indicates the systems are completely separate. Virtualization for malware analyzation is also kind of isolation.
			i. Physical Isolation and Airgaps
				□ Physical isolation ensures that network isn't connected to any network.
				□ e.g. SCADA (Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition) systems are typically industrial control systems within large facilities such as power plants or water treatment facilities. While these systems operate within their own network, it is ensured that they are isolated from any other network.
				□ This physical separation significantly reduces the risk to SCADA systems as an attacker won't be able to reach the network from internet.
				□ An airgap is metaphor for physical isolation indicating there is a gap of air between an isolated system and other systems.
				
			ii. Logical Separation and Segmentation
				□ Routers and firewalls provide basic level of separation and segmentation.
				□ Routers segment traffic between networks using rules in ACL. Administrators use subnetting to divide larger IP address ranges into smaller ranges and implement ACLs to allow or block traffic.
				□ Firewalls separate network traffic using basic packet-filtering rules and can also use more sophisticated methods to block undesirable traffic.
				□ It is possible to segment traffic between logical groups of users or computers with a VLAN (virtual local area network)
				
			iii. Comparing a Layer 2 Versus Layer 3 Switch
				□ Traditional switches are layer 2 switches and use MAC addresses in packets to determine destination port and can forward broadcast traffic to all ports on switch.
				□ Layer 3 switch mimics the behavior of a router and allows network administrators to create VLANs. As layer 3 switch forwards traffic based on destination IP instead of MAC address, it is not susceptible to ARP-based attacks.
				
			iv. Isolating Traffic with a VLAN
				□ Virtual local area networks (VLANs) separate or segment traffic on physical networks and you can create multiple VLANs with a single Layer 3 switch.
				□ VLAN tasks are
					® Segment the network 
					® Reduce collisions
					® Organize the network
					® Boost performance
					® Increase security
				□ Susceptible to VLAN hopping by switch spoofing, double  tagging.
				□ A vlan can logically group several different computers together or logically separate computers without regard to their physical location.
				□ Vlans are also used to separate traffic types, such as voice traffic on vlan and data traffic on separate vlan.
				
				
		c. Media Gateway
			§ It is a device that converts data from the format used on one network to the format used on another network.
			§ e.g. VoIP gateway converts telephony traffic between traditional phone lines and an IP-based network which helps users to make and receive phone calls using VoIP device and the gateway translate traffic and transmit calls over traditional phone line.
			§ Examine on-bound and out-bound traffic
			§ Prevents unsolicited advertisements
			§ Control phishing attempts
			§ Blocks bad attachments
			§ Sometimes have DLP installed on it
			
		d. Proxy Servers
			§ Many networks use proxy servers to forward requests for services (such as HTTPs) from clients.
			§ They can improve performance by caching content and some proxy servers can restrict users' access to inappropriate web sites by content filtering.
			§ It is located on the edge of network bordering internet and intranet.
			§ 
					
			§ Proxy servers can also proxy(apart from normal HTTP or HTTPS) for other internet protocols like FTP
			
			i. Caching Content for Performance
				□ Proxy increases the performance of the internet requests by caching(by dedicated RAM area or high-performance disk) each result received from the internet.
				□ It reduces the amount of internet bandwidth used for web browsing as page doesn't need to be retrieved again.
				
			ii. Transparent Proxy Versus Nontransparent Proxy
				□ A transparent proxy will accept and forward requests without modifying them and it is simple to set up and use and also provides caching.
				□ Non-transparent proxy server can modify or filter requests and they can also use URL filters for these purpose.
				
			iii. Reverse Proxy
				□ It accepts requests from internet, typically for a single web server.
				□ 
						
				□ It appears to clients as web server but is forwarding requests to web server and serving pages returned by the web server to protect the web server.
				□ This configuration  (proxy in DMZ acting as web server) allows the web server to be located in the private network behind a second firewall.
				□ Reverse proxy server can be used for a single server or a web farm of multiple servers. When used in the web farm, it can act as load balancer.
				
			iv. Application Proxy
				□ Application proxy is used for specific applications.
				□ It accepts requests, forwards the requests to appropriate server, and then sends the response to the original requestor.
				□ A forward proxy used for HTTP is a basic application proxy.
				
		e. Unified Threat Management (UTM)
			§ It is a single solution that combines multiple security controls.
			§ Main goal of UTM is to provide better security while simplifying management as it will administrator's workload without compromising security.
			§ UTM security appliances combine the features of multiple security solutions on to a single appliance e.g. a UTM appliance might include a firewall, antivirus protection, anti-spam protection, URL filtering, and content filtering.
			§ UTM security appliances include multiple capabilities, including:
				□ URL filtering - same job as proxy server, they block access to sites based on the URL.
				□ Malware inspection - the malware inspection component of a UTM appliance screens incoming data (as malware often comes into network via spam or malicious web pages) for known malware and blocks it.
				□ Content inspection - it includes a combination of different content filters, monitors incoming data streams and attempts to block any malicious content.it can also include spam filters.
				□ DDoS mitigator - it attempts to detect DDoS and block them. It is similar to how IPSs block attacks.
			§ A common issue with UTM is a misconfigured content filter.
			§ It is common to place UTM appliances at the network border, between intranet and internet allowing it to intercept and analyze all the traffic to from the internet. However, the placement is dependent on how the UTM appliance is being used. e.g. can be placed inside DMZ  and used as proxy server.
		f. Mail Gateways
			§ A mail gateway is a server that examines all incoming and outgoing email and attempts to reduce risks associated with email.
			§ They are located in between email server and the internet and configure it for their purposes.
			§ All the mail goes to the gateway before it goes to the email server. Additionally, many vendors include a mail gateway within a UTM appliance as security feature.
			§ Mail gateways often include data loss prevention (DLP) capabilities. They examine outgoing email looking for confidential or sensitive information and block them.
			§ Many mail gateways also support encryption and can encrypt all outgoing email to ensure confidentiality for data-in-transit or only encrypt certain data based on policies.
